From 5a9baa5041c685d10cedefcfc3c65876c41b88cf Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: Claude <noreply@anthropic.com>
Date: Sat, 1 Aug 2026 09:33:49 +0000
Subject: [PATCH] Give the JavaScript stack limit a reserve, so a catch can run
 after an overflow
MIME-Version: 1.0
Content-Type: text/plain; charset=UTF-8
Content-Transfer-Encoding: 8bit

Every real JavaScript engine lets `try { recurse() } catch (e) { report(e) }`
work. This one could not: the only limit was the CLR's own stack probe, which
fires when the stack is all but gone, and .NET runs a catch handler as a funclet
on top of the frames it is handling rather than after reclaiming them. So the
handler began life with no stack, its first call threw "Maximum call stack size
exceeded" again, and that second throw escaped the very `try` meant to catch it
and killed the process. Octane's own harness is exactly this shape —
`catch (e) { suite.NotifyError(e) }` — which is how one overflowing benchmark
took its whole suite down with it.

The funclet behaviour is .NET's, not a defect here: a plain C# program that
recurses until TryEnsureSufficientExecutionStack fails manages a recursion depth
of 1 inside its own handler, against a baseline of 257k. What engines do differ
on is WHERE the limit sits. V8 trips its own limit with a deliberate reserve of
real stack still free, and that reserve is what the handler spends. This adds
the same thing:

* JSContextOptions.MaxStackUsageBytes budgets the native stack one JavaScript
  execution may consume. It defaults to 0 — disabled, CLR probe only, byte for
  byte the historical behaviour — because a host that does not control its
  JavaScript thread's stack size cannot pick a number: too high is inert, too
  low turns legitimate deep recursion into a spurious RangeError.
* The shell now runs JavaScript on a thread whose stack size it chooses (16 MiB)
  and budgets 12 MiB of it. Owning the number is the point: the runtime's own
  stack is 1 MiB on Windows and 8 MiB on a typical Linux and is not knowable
  from managed code. The split gives ~10.6k frames of recursion before the throw
  and ~3.4k after it, where there were 0.

Two things the implementation has to get right, both found by testing:

The anchor the budget measures against is [ThreadStatic]. A context's JavaScript
does not stay on one stack — setTimeout callbacks, async continuations and
resumed generators run on pool threads — and comparing an address from one stack
against another yields a meaningless number that can exceed any budget. As an
instance field it made timer callbacks fail with a spurious RangeError that
ReportError swallowed silently; `setTimeout` stopped firing at all.

And the budget has to hand the reserve to the handler explicitly. Because the
funclet really is as deep as the throw was, measuring it against the original
anchor puts it over budget on its first call — fixing the threading bug alone
re-broke recovery. The trip point is now recorded, calls at or below it spend
the reserve, and the mark clears once execution climbs back above it, which is
precisely when the handler has returned. The CLR probe still stands behind all
of it as the hard floor.

Independent of 0046 and 0047: it shares Program.cs with 0046 but no hunk
overlaps, and it applies cleanly to the pinned pointer either on its own or
after them (both checked). Full Broiler.JS suite with all three applied:
13 projects, 7281 tests, 0 failures.

Claude-Session: https://claude.ai/code/session_014rA1kFfRgAYZjjVC7ZuPWD

Co-Authored-By: Claude <noreply@anthropic.com>
